# Copy to .env and adjust the path if your DB lives elsewhere.
# This is required for local dev - Docker sets DATABASE_URL via its own env injection.
DATABASE_URL=file:./prisma/data.db

# --- Outgoing mail (password reset + email verification codes) ---------------
# Optional. Without these the app runs normally; only the coded reset paths
# refuse, with SMTP_NOT_CONFIGURED. Plain SMTP, so any provider works.
#
# Gmail: turn on 2-Step Verification, then Google Account -> App passwords, and
# paste the 16-character code as SMTP_PASS. Your normal password will NOT work -
# Google disabled that in May 2022. A throwaway account is fine and keeps the
# credential unconnected to anything personal.
#
# Port 465 is implicit TLS; 587 is STARTTLS. The app derives which from the port,
# so do not mix them.
#
# SMTP_PASS may be pasted exactly as Google displays it, spaces and all -
# verified working, so do not "fix" it by stripping them.
#
# SMTP_FROM: the display name is always yours, but **Gmail rewrites the From
# ADDRESS to the authenticated account** unless that address is a verified
# "Send mail as" alias (Gmail -> Settings -> Accounts and Import). Setting a
# custom address here without doing that first fails SILENTLY: the mail still
# arrives, just from the Gmail account, and nothing anywhere reports it.
#
# **Do not send as noreply@<your domain> from a consumer Gmail account.**
# Considered and rejected 2026-08-16. The alias itself is free and easy - free
# Namecheap Email Forwarding delivers the one verification code - but the
# resulting mail is DKIM-signed as gmail.com while the From claims your domain,
# and consumer Gmail cannot DKIM-sign for a custom domain at all. No DNS record
# fixes that; it needs Google Workspace or a paid sending service. The trade is
# a cosmetic From address for a real chance of landing in Junk, on mail whose
# entire job is to arrive within a minute. Sending as the Gmail account keeps
# SPF and DKIM aligned and is the best free deliverability available.
#
# These must ALSO be added to the `environment:` block of docker-compose.yml,
# the same way JWT_SECRET is - otherwise the container never sees them.
#SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
#SMTP_PORT=465
#SMTP_USER=saltychart.notify@gmail.com
#SMTP_PASS=your-16-char-app-password
#SMTP_FROM=SaltyChart <saltychart.notify@gmail.com>
